%[text] # DDC Example Walkthrough: One PRI through the receiver DDC
%[text] This walkthrough follows one real ADC record through the public DDC API.
%[text] It illustrates frequency translation, two filtered rate changes, local state reset, retained startup output, and the sample-only timing contract.
%%
%[text] ## Define one aligned PRI
clc
close
clear

design = radardemo.ddc.createDesign(struct());
sampleCount = 88236;
startTick = 0;
adcRateHz = design.adcRateHz;
outputRateHz = design.outputRateHz;
chirpStartFrequencyHz = 47.5e6;
chirpEndFrequencyHz = 52.5e6;
chirpCenterFrequencyHz = 50e6;
undesiredSignalHz = 70e6;
desiredAmplitudeCounts = 18000;
undesiredSignalAmplitudeCounts = 3500;
noiseStandardDeviationCounts = 100;
globalSampleIndex = startTick + (0:sampleCount - 1).';
decimationFactors = design.decimationFactors;
combinedDecimationFactor = prod(decimationFactors);
stage1RateHz = adcRateHz / decimationFactors(1);
stage2RateHz = stage1RateHz / decimationFactors(2);
sampleIntervalSeconds = 1 / adcRateHz;
inputDurationSeconds = sampleCount * sampleIntervalSeconds;
chirpRateHzPerSecond = (chirpEndFrequencyHz - chirpStartFrequencyHz) / ...
    inputDurationSeconds;
configuration = table(["ADC input"; "FIR 1"; "After /3"; "FIR 2"; "Output"], ...
    [adcRateHz; adcRateHz; stage1RateHz; stage1RateHz; outputRateHz] / 1e6, ...
    ["Real ADC samples"; "25 taps, 25 MHz cutoff"; "Keep every third"; ...
    "241 taps, 5.625 MHz cutoff"; "Keep every fourth"], ...
    'VariableNames', ["Stage", "Rate_MSps", "Operation"]);
disp(configuration) %[output:152bc86b]
disp(table(sampleCount, combinedDecimationFactor, ... %[output:group:45796f23] %[output:6202c505]
    sampleCount / combinedDecimationFactor, inputDurationSeconds * 1e6, ... %[output:6202c505]
    'VariableNames', ["InputSamples", "Decimation", "OutputSamples", "PriDuration_us"])) %[output:group:45796f23] %[output:6202c505]
%[text] The desired linear chirp sweeps upward from 47.5 MHz at t = 0 to 52.5 MHz at the exclusive PRI endpoint T = N/Fs = 588.24 μs; its center is 50 MHz. The last ADC tick occurs one sample before T. This synthetic full-PRI chirp is not a transmit-pulse model. Complex mixing by 50 MHz translates it to a −2.5 MHz to +2.5 MHz baseband sweep centered at 0 MHz.
%[text] A real chirp also has a negative-frequency image. After mixing, that image spans −102.5 MHz to −97.5 MHz, equivalent to +47.5 MHz to +52.5 MHz at 150 MS/s. Stage 1 suppresses this image before /3.
%[text] The undesired 70 MHz interferer moves to +20 MHz; its negative component moves to −120 MHz, equivalent to +30 MHz. Stage 1 suppresses the +30 MHz component before /3. The +20 MHz component passes near FIR 1's edge, so FIR 2 must reject it before /4; otherwise +20 MHz aliases to −5 MHz at output. This one interferer illustrates the rejection path, not broadband alias rejection.
%%
%[text] ## Synthesize and quantize the real ADC input
%[text] The 70 MHz interferer is outside the desired chirp band. Add zero-mean Gaussian noise with 100 ADC-count RMS using a local fixed-seed stream, so repeated runs are reproducible without changing MATLAB global random state. Rounded int16 values model quantized counts, not calibrated volts.
timeSeconds = globalSampleIndex / adcRateHz;
chirpPhaseRadians = 2 * pi * (chirpStartFrequencyHz * timeSeconds + ...
    0.5 * chirpRateHzPerSecond * timeSeconds .^ 2);
desiredChirp = desiredAmplitudeCounts * cos(chirpPhaseRadians);
undesiredSignal = undesiredSignalAmplitudeCounts * cos( ...
    2 * pi * undesiredSignalHz * timeSeconds);
noiseStream = RandStream("mt19937ar", "Seed", 17);
adcNoise = noiseStandardDeviationCounts * ...
    randn(noiseStream, size(desiredChirp));
adcSignal = desiredChirp + undesiredSignal + adcNoise;
observedNoiseRmsCounts = sqrt(mean(adcNoise .^ 2));
adcPriSamples = int16(round(adcSignal));
adcPriDouble = double(adcPriSamples);
disp(table(chirpStartFrequencyHz / 1e6, chirpEndFrequencyHz / 1e6, ... %[output:group:90cc4624] %[output:8ffd8d87]
    chirpCenterFrequencyHz / 1e6, undesiredSignalHz / 1e6, ... %[output:8ffd8d87]
    desiredAmplitudeCounts, undesiredSignalAmplitudeCounts, ... %[output:8ffd8d87]
    noiseStandardDeviationCounts, observedNoiseRmsCounts, ... %[output:8ffd8d87]
    max(abs(adcSignal)), double(intmax("int16")), ... %[output:8ffd8d87]
    'VariableNames', ["ChirpStart_MHz", "ChirpEnd_MHz", ... %[output:8ffd8d87]
    "ChirpCenter_MHz", "UndesiredSignal_MHz", "DesiredPeak_counts", ... %[output:8ffd8d87]
    "UndesiredPeak_counts", "NoiseRMS_configured_counts", ... %[output:8ffd8d87]
    "NoiseRMS_observed_counts", "CombinedPeak_counts", ... %[output:8ffd8d87]
    "Int16PeakLimit_counts"])) %[output:group:90cc4624] %[output:8ffd8d87]
%%
%[text] ## Process one PRI with fresh mixer and filter state
%[text] Public execution boundary: one complete physical PRI per call. The API creates local oscillator, FIR-delay, and decimation-phase state for this call; no continuation state passes between calls.
[output, metadata] = radardemo.ddc.processFrame(adcPriDouble, design);
expectedOutputCount = sampleCount / combinedDecimationFactor;
assert(isequal(size(adcPriSamples), [sampleCount, 1]));
assert(isequal(size(output), [expectedOutputCount, 1]));
assert(~isreal(output));
repeatOutput = radardemo.ddc.processFrame(adcPriDouble, design);
assert(isequal(output, repeatOutput));
groupDelaySeconds = metadata.groupDelayInputSamples * sampleIntervalSeconds;
startupSpanSeconds = metadata.startupInputSamples * sampleIntervalSeconds;
disp(table(metadata.outputSampleCount, metadata.decimationFactor, ... %[output:group:295d0465] %[output:8698b576]
    metadata.groupDelayInputSamples, metadata.groupDelayOutputSamples, ... %[output:8698b576]
    groupDelaySeconds * 1e6, metadata.startupInputSamples, ... %[output:8698b576]
    metadata.startupOutputSamples, startupSpanSeconds * 1e6, ... %[output:8698b576]
    'VariableNames', ["OutputSamples", "Decimation", "DelayInputSamples", ... %[output:8698b576]
    "DelayOutputPeriods", "Delay_us", "StartupInputSamples", ... %[output:8698b576]
    "StartupOutputSamples", "Startup_us"])) %[output:group:295d0465] %[output:8698b576]
%[text] For zero-based output row q, nominal coordinate tick is startTick + 12*q − 372. Causal input support spans startTick + 12*q − 744 through startTick + 12\*q; full support first holds at q = 62.
%[text] Every produced row remains in output. No input padding, flush, or tail rows. The caller maps global PRI ticks and subtracts group delay once. Downstream range processing owns complete-window validity for startup, blanking, and PRI-end truncation.
%%
%[text] ## Inspect spectra and retained startup samples
%[text] Zero-padded FFTs show the chirp band and undesired signal; finite-record FFT levels are not calibrated amplitude or filter-response measurements. Input amplitude uses a one-sided spectrum; output uses a two-sided complex spectrum. Both spectra express magnitude in dB relative to one ADC count.
inputFftLength = 2 ^ nextpow2(sampleCount);
inputSpectrum = fft(adcPriDouble, inputFftLength) / sampleCount;
inputFrequencyHz = (0:inputFftLength / 2).' * adcRateHz / inputFftLength;
inputMagnitudeCounts = 2 * abs(inputSpectrum(1:inputFftLength / 2 + 1));
inputMagnitudeCounts([1, end]) = inputMagnitudeCounts([1, end]) / 2;
steadyOutput = output(metadata.startupOutputSamples + 1:end);
outputFftLength = 2 ^ nextpow2(numel(steadyOutput));
outputSpectrum = fftshift(fft(steadyOutput, outputFftLength)) / numel(steadyOutput);
outputFrequencyHz = (-outputFftLength / 2:outputFftLength / 2 - 1).' * outputRateHz / outputFftLength;
outputMagnitudeCounts = abs(outputSpectrum);
instantaneousFrequencyHz = angle(output(2:end) .* conj(output(1:end - 1))) * ...
    outputRateHz / (2 * pi);
frequencyIntervalIndex = (0:numel(instantaneousFrequencyHz) - 1).';
expectedInstantaneousFrequencyHz = chirpStartFrequencyHz - ...
    design.mixerFrequencyHz + chirpRateHzPerSecond * ...
    (12 * frequencyIntervalIndex + 6 - metadata.groupDelayInputSamples) / adcRateHz;
earlyWindow = frequencyIntervalIndex >= 500 & frequencyIntervalIndex <= 650;
lateWindow = frequencyIntervalIndex >= 6500 & frequencyIntervalIndex <= 6650;
earlyMeasuredFrequencyHz = mean(instantaneousFrequencyHz(earlyWindow));
earlyExpectedFrequencyHz = mean(expectedInstantaneousFrequencyHz(earlyWindow));
lateMeasuredFrequencyHz = mean(instantaneousFrequencyHz(lateWindow));
lateExpectedFrequencyHz = mean(expectedInstantaneousFrequencyHz(lateWindow));
earlyFrequencyErrorHz = earlyMeasuredFrequencyHz - earlyExpectedFrequencyHz;
lateFrequencyErrorHz = lateMeasuredFrequencyHz - lateExpectedFrequencyHz;
assert(abs(earlyFrequencyErrorHz) <= 2e3);
assert(abs(lateFrequencyErrorHz) <= 2e3);
inputMagnitudeDb = 20 * log10(max(inputMagnitudeCounts, eps));
outputMagnitudeDb = 20 * log10(max(outputMagnitudeCounts, eps));
spectraFigure = figure("Name", "DDC input and output spectra", "Color", "w", ... %[output:267ab97a]
    "Position", [1, 1, 1100, 750], "WindowStyle", "docked"); %[output:267ab97a]
tiledlayout(2, 1); %[output:267ab97a]
nexttile; %[output:267ab97a]
plot(inputFrequencyHz / 1e6, inputMagnitudeDb); %[output:267ab97a]
xlim([0, adcRateHz / 2e6]); %[output:267ab97a]
grid on; %[output:267ab97a]
title("Real input PRI: chirp and undesired 70 MHz signal"); %[output:267ab97a]
xlabel("Frequency (MHz)"); %[output:267ab97a]
ylabel("Magnitude (dB)"); %[output:267ab97a]
nexttile; %[output:267ab97a]
plot(outputFrequencyHz / 1e6, outputMagnitudeDb); %[output:267ab97a]
xlim([-outputRateHz / 2e6, outputRateHz / 2e6]); %[output:267ab97a]
grid on; %[output:267ab97a]
title("Complex output: translated chirp band and interferer rejection"); %[output:267ab97a]
xlabel("Frequency (MHz)"); %[output:267ab97a]
ylabel("Magnitude (dB)"); %[output:267ab97a]
xline((chirpStartFrequencyHz - design.mixerFrequencyHz) / 1e6, "--"); %[output:267ab97a]
xline((chirpEndFrequencyHz - design.mixerFrequencyHz) / 1e6, "--"); %[output:267ab97a]
%[text] The output spectrum excludes the first 62 rows because local FIR history fills during startup; the output array retains them. The chirp should span approximately −2.5 MHz to +2.5 MHz. FFT leakage broadens the edges, and the remaining interferer level is an illustration rather than a broadband rejection measurement. Window means of adjacent phase increments average random noise variation in the early and late chirp checks.
disp(table(earlyMeasuredFrequencyHz / 1e6, earlyExpectedFrequencyHz / 1e6, ... %[output:group:79972a68] %[output:2ca26b5b]
    earlyFrequencyErrorHz / 1e3, lateMeasuredFrequencyHz / 1e6, ... %[output:2ca26b5b]
    lateExpectedFrequencyHz / 1e6, lateFrequencyErrorHz / 1e3, ... %[output:2ca26b5b]
    'VariableNames', ["EarlyMeasured_MHz", "EarlyExpected_MHz", ... %[output:2ca26b5b]
    "EarlyError_kHz", "LateMeasured_MHz", "LateExpected_MHz", ... %[output:2ca26b5b]
    "LateError_kHz"])) %[output:group:79972a68] %[output:2ca26b5b]
%%
%[text] ## Inspect startup I and Q
%[text] I and Q are signed sample values, so this time-domain plot remains linear.
startupPlotSampleCount = min(4 * metadata.startupOutputSamples, metadata.outputSampleCount);
startupSampleIndex = (0:startupPlotSampleCount - 1).';
startupFigure = figure("Name", "DDC startup", "Color", "w", ... %[output:2508c4aa]
    "Position", [1, 1, 1100, 750], "WindowStyle", "docked"); %[output:2508c4aa]
plot(startupSampleIndex, real(output(1:startupPlotSampleCount)), ... %[output:2508c4aa]
    startupSampleIndex, imag(output(1:startupPlotSampleCount))); %[output:2508c4aa]
xline(metadata.startupOutputSamples - 0.5, "--", "Steady-state analysis begins"); %[output:2508c4aa]
grid on; %[output:2508c4aa]
title("Startup I/Q samples; all rows remain in output"); %[output:2508c4aa]
xlabel("Output sample index (zero-based)"); %[output:2508c4aa]
ylabel("Complex output (ADC counts)"); %[output:2508c4aa]
legend("I", "Q", "Location", "best"); %[output:2508c4aa]
%%
%[text] ## Inspect wideband FIR magnitude responses
%[text] Each panel shows one FIR at the rate where it operates, before its decimator. The horizontal axis is baseband physical frequency after mixing; the marked ±5 MHz band is wanted signal to preserve. Stage 1 runs at 150 MS/s, so ±25 MHz marks the post-/3 Nyquist boundary, not a claimed 60 dB stopband edge. Stage 2 runs at 50 MS/s, so ±6.25 MHz marks its stopband start and the output Nyquist boundary. These boundaries explain where content can fold after rate reduction; they are not standalone multirate acceptance tests. The plotted response is FIR magnitude only.
%[text] Read the wideband shape first: near 0 dB, sinusoid amplitudes are preserved; the sloping transition leads into a stopband where negative dB values indicate suppression. FIR 1 has a broad transition around its 25 MHz cutoff, while FIR 2 has a narrower transition around 5.625 MHz. The display clips magnitudes below −140 dB; those flat bottoms are a plotting floor, not measured rejection limits. These are magnitude responses, not phase or delay plots.
stage1FrequencyHz = linspace(-design.adcRateHz / 2, ...
    design.adcRateHz / 2, 16385);
stage2FrequencyHz = linspace(-design.intermediateRateHz / 2, ...
    design.intermediateRateHz / 2, 16385);
stage1WidebandResponse = freqz(design.stage1Numerator, 1, ...
    2 * pi * stage1FrequencyHz / design.adcRateHz);
stage2WidebandResponse = freqz(design.stage2Numerator, 1, ...
    2 * pi * stage2FrequencyHz / design.intermediateRateHz);
stage1WidebandMagnitudeDb = 20 * log10(max(abs(stage1WidebandResponse), 1e-7));
stage2WidebandMagnitudeDb = 20 * log10(max(abs(stage2WidebandResponse), 1e-7));
widebandFigure = figure("Name", "DDC wideband FIR magnitude responses", ... %[output:5ef7fbe5]
    "Color", "w", "Position", [1, 1, 1100, 750], "WindowStyle", "docked"); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
tiledlayout(2, 1); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
nexttile; %[output:5ef7fbe5]
widebandLine = plot(stage1FrequencyHz / 1e6, stage1WidebandMagnitudeDb); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
wantedBandLines = xline([-5, 5], "--"); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
nyquistLines = xline([-25, 25], ":"); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
ylim([-140, 5]); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
xlim([-design.adcRateHz / 2, design.adcRateHz / 2] / 1e6); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
grid on; %[output:5ef7fbe5]
title("Stage 1 FIR at 150 MS/s"); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
xlabel("Baseband frequency after mixing (MHz)"); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
ylabel("FIR magnitude (dB)"); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
legend([widebandLine, wantedBandLines(1), nyquistLines(1)], ... %[output:5ef7fbe5]
    ["FIR magnitude", "Wanted-band edges", "Post-/3 Nyquist"], "Location", "south"); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
nexttile; %[output:5ef7fbe5]
widebandLine = plot(stage2FrequencyHz / 1e6, stage2WidebandMagnitudeDb); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
wantedBandLines = xline([-5, 5], "--"); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
nyquistLines = xline([-6.25, 6.25], ":"); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
ylim([-140, 5]); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
xlim([-design.intermediateRateHz / 2, design.intermediateRateHz / 2] / 1e6); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
grid on; %[output:5ef7fbe5]
title("Stage 2 FIR at 50 MS/s"); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
xlabel("Baseband frequency after mixing (MHz)"); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
ylabel("FIR magnitude (dB)"); %[output:5ef7fbe5]
legend([widebandLine, wantedBandLines(1), nyquistLines(1)], ... %[output:group:20a83f5f] %[output:5ef7fbe5]
    ["FIR magnitude", "Wanted-band edges", "Stopband start / output Nyquist"], "Location", "south"); %[output:group:20a83f5f] %[output:5ef7fbe5]
%%
%[text] ## Inspect the principal passband response
%[text] This plot shows how the two FIRs and their cascade change the amplitudes of wanted frequencies between −5 and +5 MHz. We inspect it to check that filtering preserves the wanted waveform with very little frequency-dependent gain variation, rather than judging rejection of unwanted frequencies.
%[text] The public response API separately evaluates the designed filters and cascade on its fixed acceptance grid; the table reports those unchanged metrics. In the zoom, 0 dB means unity magnitude, and cascade magnitudes multiply, so their dB values add. The small in-band variation is passband ripple. A scientific-axis multiplier can make a tiny ripple easy to misread, so this view forces ordinary decimal dB ticks.
%[text] This zoom asks what the filtering costs the wanted signal: frequency-dependent gain can reshape a waveform even when rejection is excellent. The cascade budget is at most 0.1 dB passband ripple and at least 60 dB digital alias rejection under the named metric profile. The first requirement concerns preservation; the second needs the separate alias and stopband calculations, not this passband-only plot.
%[text] A real cosine with peak amplitude A produces complex envelope A/2 before the approved final ×2 compensation, which is not included in these FIR responses. Output returns toward A, subject to FIR gain, phase, and residual image. The ×2 gain doubles noise amplitude without improving SNR or calibrating counts as volts.
responseMetrics = radardemo.ddc.measureResponse(design, struct());
assert(responseMetrics.accepted);
disp(table(responseMetrics.responseMetricProfile, responseMetrics.passbandRippleDb, ... %[output:group:28e70666] %[output:7231ad24]
    responseMetrics.stage2AliasRejectionDb, responseMetrics.digitalAliasRejectionDb, ... %[output:7231ad24]
    'VariableNames', ["MetricProfile", "PassbandRipple_dB", ... %[output:7231ad24]
    "Stage2AliasRejection_dB", "CascadeAliasRejection_dB"])) %[output:group:28e70666] %[output:7231ad24]
responseFrequencyMHz = responseMetrics.frequencyHz / 1e6;
passbandMask = abs(responseMetrics.frequencyHz) <= 5e6;
principalResponses = [responseMetrics.stage1Response, ...
    responseMetrics.stage2Response, responseMetrics.principalCascadeResponse];
principalMagnitudeDb = 20 * log10(max(abs(principalResponses), 1e-12));
responseFigure = figure("Name", "DDC principal passband response", ... %[output:73ec6a63]
    "Color", "w", "Position", [1, 1, 1100, 750], "WindowStyle", "docked"); %[output:73ec6a63]
plot(responseFrequencyMHz(passbandMask), principalMagnitudeDb(passbandMask, :)); %[output:73ec6a63]
grid on; %[output:73ec6a63]
title("Principal passband response"); %[output:73ec6a63]
xlabel("Frequency (MHz)"); %[output:73ec6a63]
ylabel("Magnitude (dB)"); %[output:73ec6a63]
passbandAxes = gca;
passbandAxes.YAxis.Exponent = 0;
legend("Stage 1", "Stage 2", "Cascade", "Location", "best"); %[output:73ec6a63]
%%
%[text] ## Inspect cascade alias envelope
%[text] At each output frequency in the wanted band, this plot shows the strongest individual unwanted alias-path gain through both FIR stages, relative to the peak wanted-path gain. We inspect it to check that unwanted frequencies folding into the wanted band are sufficiently suppressed, with the envelope remaining below the −60 dB line.
branchPairs = responseMetrics.cascadeBranchPairs;
principalBranch = branchPairs(:, 1) == 0 & branchPairs(:, 2) == 0;
%[text] The cascade alias plot shows only the nonprincipal branch envelope. Full acceptance also includes the direct principal response over the specified stopband; the metric includes that term, but this figure does not draw it.
aliasBranches = responseMetrics.cascadeAliasBranches(passbandMask, ~principalBranch);
aliasEnvelope = max(abs(aliasBranches), [], 2) / responseMetrics.principalPassbandGain;
aliasFigure = figure("Name", "DDC cascade alias envelope", ... %[output:6a0d8d57]
    "Color", "w", "Position", [1, 1, 1100, 750], "WindowStyle", "docked"); %[output:6a0d8d57]
plot(responseFrequencyMHz(passbandMask), 20 * log10(max(aliasEnvelope, 1e-12))); %[output:6a0d8d57]
yline(-60, "--", "60 dB acceptance"); %[output:6a0d8d57]
grid on; %[output:6a0d8d57]
title("Cascade alias envelope relative to peak passband"); %[output:6a0d8d57]
xlabel("Output frequency (MHz)"); %[output:6a0d8d57]
ylabel("Relative alias magnitude (dB)"); %[output:6a0d8d57]
%%
%[text] ## Interpret gain and evidence
%[text] The response plots show designed-filter behavior. Input and output FFTs show finite-record chirp and interferer content, including leakage. This one-PRI example does not establish full receiver detection, range-window validity, hardware behavior, or real-time performance.

%[appendix]{"version":"1.0"}
